Pokeri 0.1 - initial Amiga release (29 September 2026)
===================================================

An Amiga port of the Finnish RAY video poker game, by Vesuri.
The original 68008 program runs natively; the Hitachi display and AY sound
hardware are implemented using Amiga planar graphics, the blitter and Paula.

Requirements
------------
PAL Amiga with a 68020 or better. A1200 with Fast RAM is recommended.
The WHDLoad slave reserves 1 MB Chip RAM and 4 MB Fast RAM, plus 512 KB
for Kickstart. Allow additional memory for Workbench, WHDLoad and PRELOAD;
2 MB Chip / 8 MB Fast is the release test configuration.
ECS rendering is supported; A500 performance tuning remains unfinished.
No FPU, SDL, Musashi or software-math libraries are needed.

Install WHDLoad 17 or newer and Commodore Installer 43 or newer first.
WHDLoad needs a Kickstart 3.1 image and its matching RTB file in
Devs:Kickstarts/ or WHDCOMMON:. Supported pairs:
  kick40068.A1200 / kick40068.A1200.RTB
  kick40068.A4000 / kick40068.A4000.RTB
  kick40063.A600  / kick40063.A600.RTB
Obtain WHDLoad and RTB files from https://www.whdload.de/ . Supply your own
Kickstart image. WHDLoad, Installer and Kickstart are not included.

You must supply these four original ROM dumps, each exactly 65536 bytes:
  77POK30   77POK38   77POK34   PARA200J
The original ROM files are not included. No other ROM revisions are supported.

Installation
------------
Unpack Pokeri-0.1.lha and double-click Install. Choose the destination parent
drawer and, if needed, the drawer containing the four uncompressed ROMs.
If Pokeri already exists, choose Keep to preserve ROMs and saved state, or
Remove to delete that entire installation. When all ROMs are already in its
data drawer, the installer does not ask for their location again.
Start using the installed Pokeri icon. Keep the installed NoVBRMove and NoWriteCache tooltypes: the native runner
uses trace exceptions, and saves need direct writes for reliable exit.
For a Shell launch use:
  WHDLoad Pokeri.slave PRELOAD NOVBRMOVE NOWRITECACHE

Manual layout:
  Pokeri.slave
  Pokeri.info           (WHDLoad icon)
  ReadMe
  data/Pokeri           (executable)
  data/77POK30, data/77POK38, data/77POK34, data/PARA200J

Standalone AmigaDOS: change into data, then run Pokeri. Alternatively keep
Pokeri in a drawer and put the chips in its data subdrawer or beside it.
The loader searches data/, current drawer, then legacy rom/, in that order.
Always launch from the intended game drawer; saves use the current drawer.
ROMs are loaded from disk on each launch and are never modified.

Controls
--------
C              Insert coin
Space          Deal / draw
1-5            Hold the corresponding card
D              Double a winning hand
Left / Right   Big / Small when doubling
Esc or left mouse button  Quit normally, saving state
F1             Service door
F2             Operator control
F3             Lamp panel

Saving
------
Normal exit saves nvram.bin (battery-backed hardware state) and accounting.bin
(retained credits/accounting). They are created automatically in data/ under
WHDLoad. Keep both to retain your machine state. Existing images are backed
up as nvram.bak and accounting.bak before replacement. Do not make the data
drawer read-only. To restore a backup, quit and copy the .bak over its .bin.
The normal AmigaDOS path also uses temporary .new files during replacement.
WHDLoad uses supported file writes instead of unsupported rename operations.
Always use Esc or the left mouse button for a saving exit, not reset.
WHDLoad's external quit key is unavailable with NoVBRMove.
A fresh installation initializes itself and starts with zero credits.

Initial release limitations
---------------------------
This is a playable development release, not a claim of sustained 50 FPS.
Startup and drawing bursts are still being optimized. AY envelopes follow
PAL time, but graphics/command processing can delay subsequent sound commands.
Real-machine colour/audio calibration and A500 speed tuning remain open.
Known original artwork quirks, including stripe alignment, are preserved.

Source and reports: https://github.com/Vesuri/raypokeri
Original game and artwork: RAY. Amiga port: Vesa Halttunen / Vesuri.
WHDLoad kickemu/kickfs by Bert Jahn and contributors (public-domain SDK).
Installer/icon conventions follow the WHDLoad template and the author's
Rescue on Fractalus, Vette! and Slicks ports.
